Group Name: 01_04
TASK 1: Conceptualisation & Pseudocode (20%) (Maximum 2 pages)
1.1 Understanding the Problem
Explain how your group understands the "Prime-Dense Window" problem.
Our group understands the “Prime-Dense Window” problem as a string processing and mathematical validation challenge. We are required to slide a window of length W across a numeric string to find the window position that returns the highest count of unique prime numbers. The sliding window starts at index 0, comprising of the first W characters of the string, and moves one position at a time until it reaches the last valid position at index (length of string – W). At each position, the algorithm processes the present window to find every unique primes. In each window, all possible contiguous substrings are obtained. Each substring is changed to an integer and compared against two constraints: the number must be strictly less than N and it must not have already been discovered in the present window. This guarantees uniqueness, requiring a data structure efficient enough to track previously identified primes.
Primality is decided using trial division and prime numbers are tested by checking divisibility from 2 up to the square root of the number. The algorithm employs a tie-breaking rule: if more than one window share the same highest prime count, choose the window with the lowest starting index. The output adheres to a strict format: “Starting Index, Max Count: Prime numbers”, where the primes are arranged in ascending order. If 6 or more primes are discovered, the first three smallest and the last three highest are only displayed. If there are no primes discovered, the output is “Index, 0: No primes found”. If the input string has non-numeric characters, the output is “0, 0: Invalid input”.
1.2 New Test Cases
1.3 Pseudocode
